Suppose that Aumann, Bill, and Charlie rolled the numbers $a, b$, and $c$, respectively. Call what Aumann, Bill, and Charlie say for the $k$ th time $A_{k}, B_{k}$, and $C_{k}$ (for $k=1,2,3$ ). Note that $A_{1}$ is a plus whichever of $b$ and $c$ Aumann saw plus 3.5 (the expected value of the third roll), and so everyone can deduce the sum of the two numbers that Aumann saw, and similarly for Bob and Charlie. Case 1: It is not the case that $A_{1}=B_{1}=C_{1}$. Without loss of generality, assume that $A_{1} 
