Solution: We start by writing the prime factorizations of $20^{12}$ and $\left(20^{12}\right)^{2}$.

$$
\begin{gathered}
20^{12}=2^{24} \times 5^{12} \\
\left(20^{12}\right)^{2}=2^{48} \times 5^{24}
\end{gathered}
$$

$\left(20^{12}\right)^{2}$ has $49 \times 25=1225$ factors, and $20^{12}$ has $25 \times 13=325$ factors. Aside from $20^{12}$, each factor of $\left(20^{12}\right)^{2}$ that is greater than $20^{12}$ can be paired up with a factor that is less than $20^{12}$,

## PUM $\alpha \mathbb{C} 2012$

so $\left(20^{12}\right)^{2}$ has 612 factors less than $20^{12}$. Then we see that $612-324=\mathbf{2 8 8}$ of those factors are not factors of $20^{12}$.

Solution: We use the Euclidean Algorithm to solve this problem.
$$
\operatorname{gcd}\left\{n+x^{2}, n+(x+1)^{2}\right\}=\operatorname{gcd}\left\{n+x^{2}, n+x^{2}+2 x+1\right\}=\operatorname{gcd}\left\{n+x^{2}, 2 x+1\right\}
$$

## PUM $\alpha \mathbb{C} 2012$

Note that $2 x+1$ is odd, so multiplying $n+x^{2}$ by 4 will not change the greatest common divisor. Then we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \operatorname{gcd}\left\{n+x^{2}, 2 x+1\right\}=\operatorname{gcd}\left\{4 n+4 x^{2}, 2 x+1\right\}=\operatorname{gcd}\left\{4 n+(2 x+1)^{2}-4 x-1,2 x+1\right\}=\operatorname{gcd}\{4 n-4 x-1,2 x+1\} \\
& \operatorname{gcd}\{4 n-4 x-1,2 x+1\}=\operatorname{gcd}\{4 n+1,2 x+1\}
\end{aligned}
$$

Then the calculations are simple. $\operatorname{gcd}\{8005,4005\}=5, \operatorname{gcd}\{8045,2025\}=5$, and $\operatorname{gcd}\{8085,2045\}=$ 5 , so the product is $5 \times 5 \times 5=\mathbf{1 2 5}$.

We know that there are no double-edges, by Problem 5.2.4. Suppose the branching node is $z$, and it is joined to $x_{n}, w_{m}, v_{p}$ where each of those are connected to a line going to 1 such as $x_{n-1}, \ldots, x_{1}$. We create the "super-vertices" $x, w$ and $v$ as specified earlier. Each is orthogonal to the others. Normalize them to $x^{\prime}, w^{\prime}, v^{\prime}$. We can give span $\{z, x, w, v\}$ the basis $\left\{z_{0}, x^{\prime}, w^{\prime}, v^{\prime}\right\}$ for $z_{0}$ a unit vector not orthogonal to $z$ but orthogonal to each of $x^{\prime}, w^{\prime}, v^{\prime}$. It is rather readily seen that $z=\left\langle z, x^{\prime}\right\rangle x^{\prime}+\left\langle z, w^{\prime}\right\rangle w^{\prime}+\left\langle z, v^{\prime}\right\rangle v^{\prime}+\left\langle z, z_{0}\right\rangle z_{0}$. $z$ too is a unit vector (since it lies in a normalized base). Taking another inner product with $z$ gives $1=\left\langle z, x^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}+\left\langle z, w^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}+\left\langle z, v^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}+\left\langle z, z_{0}\right\rangle^{2}$, i.e. $\left\langle z, x^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}+\left\langle z, w^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}+\left\langle z, v^{\prime}\right\rangle^{2}<1\left(0<\left\langle z, z_{0}\right\rangle<\right.$ 1 ). We've already computed the inner products of $x, w, v$ with themselves, and from the Dynkin diagram we know that $\left\langle z, n x_{n}\right\rangle=\frac{n^{2}}{4}$ (since they're connected by a single edge). Substituting: $\frac{2 n^{2}}{4 n(n+1)}+\frac{2 m^{2}}{4 m(m+1)}+\frac{2 p^{2}}{4 p(p+1)}<1$, and rearranging gets $\frac{1}{n+1}+\frac{1}{m+1}+\frac{1}{p+1}>1$. Without loss of generality take $n \geq m \geq p$. Then $\frac{1}{n+1} \leq \frac{1}{m+1} \leq \frac{1}{p+1} \leq \frac{1}{2}$, or $1<\frac{3}{p+1}$, so $p<2$, i.e. $p=1$. Then, $m<3$, i.e. $m=1$, 2 . If $m=2$ then $n<5$; otherwise $n$ can be any natural.

