(ii) draw the Dynkin diagram for $\mathfrak{s l}_{n}$
(iii) draw the Dynkin diagram for $\mathfrak{s} \mathfrak{p}_{2 n}$
and show your reasoning. In particular, many details of the computations were omitted above, but you will be expected to (briefly) justify the numbers you find.

## Solution:

(i) As $L$ 's elements satisfy $x^{T} R_{2 n}+R_{2 n} x=0$, we know that $x$ can be written in the form $\left[\begin{array}{ccc}0 & C^{T} & -B^{T} \\ B & M & P \\ -C & Q & -M^{T}\end{array}\right]$ where $B$ and $C$ each have $n$ rows and 1 column and for any $n \times n$ matrix $M$ and $n \times n$ matrices $P$ and $Q$ both equal to their negative transpose.
$H$ is the set of diagonal matrices in $L$, so $H=\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n} c_{i}\left(e_{i, i}-e_{i+n, i+n}\right) \mid c_{i} \in \mathbb{C}\right\}$ (indexed from zero this time). We can give $L$ the additional basis elements $m_{i, j}= e_{i, j}-e_{j+n, i+n}(1 \leq i \neq j \leq n), p_{i, j}=e_{i, j+n}-e_{j, i+n}(1 \leq i<j \leq n), p_{i, j}^{T}$, $B_{i}=e_{i, 0}-e_{0, i+n}(1 \leq i \leq n)$ and $C_{i}=e_{0, i}-e_{i+n, 0}(1 \leq i \leq n)$ to go along with those of $H$. We find, for an arbitrary element $h \in H$ of the form specified in $H$ 's specification,

$$
\begin{aligned}
{\left[h, m_{i, j}\right] } & =\left(c_{i}-c_{j}\right) m_{i, j} \\
{\left[h, p_{i, j}\right] } & =\left(c_{i}+c_{j}\right) p_{i, j} \\
{\left[h, p_{i, j}^{T}\right] } & =-\left(c_{i}+c_{j}\right) p_{i, j}^{T} \\
{\left[h, B_{i}\right] } & =c_{i} B_{i} \\
{\left[h, C_{i}\right] } & =-c_{i} C_{i}
\end{aligned}
$$

so our roots are:

| root | eigenspace |
| :---: | :---: |
| $\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{j}$ | $\operatorname{span}\left\{m_{i, j}, m_{j, i}\right\}$ |
| $\lambda_{i}+\lambda_{j}$ | $\operatorname{span}\left\{p_{i, j}, p_{j, i}\right\}$ |
| $\lambda_{i}$ | $\operatorname{span}\left\{B_{i}\right\}$ |
| $-\lambda_{i}$ | $\operatorname{span}\left\{C_{i}\right\}$ |

For $H^{*}$, a vector space having the basis $\left\{\lambda_{i}\right\}$, we see that $\left\{\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}, \lambda_{2}-\lambda_{3}, \ldots, \lambda_{n-1}-\right. \left.\lambda_{n}, \lambda_{n}\right\}$ is a base; this base will be what we use to build our Dynkin diagram. We set $\alpha_{i}=\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{i+1}$ and $\beta=\lambda_{n}$. When $i<n$ if $e_{\alpha_{i}}=m_{i, i+1}$ then $h_{\alpha_{i}}=e_{i, i}-e_{i+n, i+n}- e_{i+1, i+1}+e_{i+n+1, i+n+1} \cdot e_{\beta}=B_{n}$ gets $h_{\beta}=2\left(e_{n, n}-e_{2 n-2 n}\right)$. We compute

$$
\begin{aligned}
{\left[h_{\alpha_{j}}, e_{\alpha_{i}}\right] } & =\left\{\begin{array}{cl}
2 e_{\alpha_{j}} & i=j \\
-e_{\alpha_{j}} & |i-j|=1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right. \\
{\left[h_{\beta}, e_{\alpha_{i}}\right] } & =\left\{\begin{array}{cl}
-2 e_{\alpha_{i}} & i=n-1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right. \\
{\left[h_{\alpha_{i}}, e_{\beta}\right] } & =\left\{\begin{array}{cl}
-e_{\beta} & i=n-1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right.
\end{aligned}
$$

Computation gives that

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left\langle\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{i+1}, \lambda_{j}-\lambda_{j+1}\right\rangle & =\left\{\begin{array}{rl}
2 & i=j \\
-1 & |i-j|=1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right. \\
\left\langle\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{i+1}, \lambda_{n}\right\rangle & =\left\{\begin{array}{rl}
-2 & i=n-1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right. \\
\left\langle\lambda_{n}, \lambda_{i}-\lambda_{i+1}\right\rangle & =\left\{\begin{array}{rl}
-1 & i=n-1 \\
0 & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right.
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $e(v, w)=\langle v, w\rangle \cdot\langle w, v\rangle$, our Dynkin diagram is:
