For $H^{*}$, a vector space having the basis $\left\{\lambda_{i}\right\}$, we see that $\left\{\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}, \lambda_{2}-\lambda_{3}, \ldots, \lambda_{n-1}-\right. \left.\lambda_{n}, 2 \lambda_{n}\right\}$ is a base; this base will be what we use to build our Dynkin diagram. We set $\alpha_{i}=\lambda_{i}-\lambda_{i+1}$ and $\beta=2 \lambda_{n}$. When $i<n$ if $e_{\alpha_{i}}=m_{i, i+1}$ then $h_{\alpha_{i}}=e_{i, i}-e_{i+n, i+n}- e_{i+1, i+1}+e_{i+n+1, i+n+1} \cdot e_{\beta}=p_{n, n}$ gets $h_{\beta}=e_{n, n}-e_{2 n, 2 n}$. We compute

